  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p>At 13,179 feet, <strong>Cloud Peak</strong> is a summit in Wyoming. With roughly 7,077 feet of topographic prominence, it stands well above the surrounding terrain. The Cloud Peak Trail covers 16.2 miles round trip (out-and-back) with about 1,683 feet of elevation gain \u2014 gentle grades, but the mileage is what makes it a Strenuous outing.</p>\n  <h3>The Route</h3><p>Most of the climbing comes early \u2014 the first half of the route does the bulk of the work before the grade eases. The steepest stretch arrives around mile 3.47, gaining roughly 600 feet per mile \u2014 expect a slow, deliberate pace through here. From the trailhead at about 9,242 feet, the route tops out near 10,820 feet. You'll cross above treeline on the way up, so the upper route is exposed to weather \u2014 check the forecast and turn around if conditions build.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=44.33799,-107.06708\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">44.33799, -107.06708</a> (tap for driving directions). It's roughly 120 miles southeast of Billings, Montana. Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>The reliable snow-free window is roughly July through September. At this elevation, afternoon thunderstorms are a serious summer hazard \u2014 start early and plan to be off the upper mountain by midday. Outside that window expect snow travel and bring traction and navigation skills to match.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Strenuous rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 6.0 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n</div>",
